cle><article class="claim" id="critical-bifurcation-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>New stable phase equilibria appear beyond the curved-orbit threshold</h2><p>The exact stationary energy yields six balanced minima, twelve index-one saddles and eight index-two saddles for sufficiently small positive coefficient excess. These counts retain vertex labels and describe phase-amplitude patterns on the fixed carrier. The scalar local diagram is complete; the higher-rank theorem concerns embeddings of these branches.</p><p>A reversing symmetry supplies the exact scalar instability counts and leading soft frequencies, including Gauss backreaction. The balanced branches persist as full-rank stable orbits near threshold, while the original orbit remains unstable above its previous boundary. Energy conservation rules out attraction to the minima; a sourced preparation or selection law remains open. 66 exact constructor checks and twelve full stationary-energy solutions with 42-pair spectra passed. No numerical bound on the theorem's neighborhood radius or new dynamical trajectory is claimed. Independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#critical-bifurcation-v01">Read the bifurcation theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="residual-gauss-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The final residual Gauss constraint is explicit</h2><p>The extra-color oscillator list now has its admissibility rule: opposite oscillator orientations must balance in total amplitude, and the full matrix charge must cancel with the unused vacuum commutators. A weighted-shift construction completes every trace-balanced color state with one vacuum oscillator, with an energy bound that is exact at r=3.</p><p>At r=2 the color quotient is homeomorphic to the cone of complex rank-one 28×28 matrices; its inherited reduced Poisson structure is retained. The full open stratum has 222 real dimensions and the full stabilizer stratum 112. A free zero-moment witness gives principal dimension 26r²+60r-2 at every finite rank. The standard local symplectic reduction theorem supplies the exact constraint germ; displayed observable frequencies are quadratic dynamics only. 70 exact checks and a full matrix cubic-error diagnostic passed. The previous stability interval is unchanged. Independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#residual-gauss-v01">Read the residual Gauss theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="higher-rank-curved-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Higher-rank color stability closes for the embedded family</h2><p>The additional color slice has positive mass and matter stiffness. Its magnetic Hessian vanishes by an exact two-color determinant identity. Positive unused-block cycle stiffness then extends the scalar energy minimum, including its quartic endpoint, to all finite ranks.</p><p>For r≥2 the sharp interval is 0&lt;κ≤rκ*(N). Zero stiffness is unstable by exact unused-color holonomy drift, whereas the scalar r=1 interval still includes zero. The proof covers the full fixed-norm surface without assuming a smooth residual gauge quotient. 57 exact constructor checks, rank-two/rank-three matrix fixtures and two full nonlinear rank-two campaigns passed. Unchanged candidate action; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#higher-rank-curved-v01">Read the full-rank stability theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="curved-modes-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The scalar curved branch now has a sharp stability boundary</h2><p>Gauss and phase reduction split the fixed-norm scalar dynamics into 14 neutral matter oscillator pairs and 28 coupled link–charge pairs. Exact symmetry sections specify every frequency and all five stiffness crossings. The first crossing gives κ*/N≈0.03760248457, about 29% above the earlier conservative bound.</p><p>The orbit remains nonlinearly stable at equality: a matter-relaxed quartic is positive, with its two symmetric coefficients rigorously enclosed by integer arithmetic. Above the boundary a real growing mode produces constrained nonlinear departure. The action is unchanged; additional color directions at higher rank remain open. 47 exact checks, four numerical linear benchmarks and three nonlinear campaigns; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#curved-modes-v01">Read the reduced modes and endpoint proof →</a></article><article class="claim" id="curved-ground-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>A stable matter-bearing background in the same action</h2><p>The completed flat-family obstruction led to a different exact branch: U=-ib, E=0 and equal positive lowest-mode matter, with every diamond holonomy -1. The scalar orbit is nonlinearly stable for 0≤κ&lt;Nρ(10-√10)/(120(6+√2)), where ρ=√(8+2√10) and each species norm is N. The bound is sufficient and conservative.</p><p>A separate compactness argument establishes a stable global minimizing set at every finite rank. It proves neither individual-orbit stability at higher rank nor attraction. The old flat-family instability is preserved. 40 exact constructor checks and two finite nonlinear numerical campaigns passed; no experimental data. Independent review pending, physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#curved-ground-v01">Read the curved-orbit stability theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="resonance-closure-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The six open resonances now have nonlinear witnesses</h2><p>Exact fixed-point sectors of the full nonlinear flow exclude the resonant modes while retaining a nonzero averaged matter drift. S3 closes t=2/3, 8/3 and 16/(4±√2); C4 closes 16/(6±√2). No detuning or action change is used.</p><p>The nonzero neutral flat family is therefore nonlinearly orbitally unstable across a&gt;0, κ≥0 at finite rank, conditional on the declared action and the preceding theorem. Positive-stiffness empty vacuum stability is preserved; other backgrounds remain unclassified. 104 exact checks and six numerical campaigns; independent review pending; physical promotion 0. Earlier dated entries below preserve their historical boundaries.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#resonance-closure-v01">Read the six-case closure theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="nonlinear-orbital-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>A nonlinear departure theorem with six open resonances</h2><p>Within the unchanged candidate action, fixed-norm Gauss-admissible perturbations can approach the nonzero flat stationary orbit while exact trajectories reach fixed higher-simplex matter stock on O(1/ε) time. The proof uses conserved co-moving matter coherence, exact Coulomb dynamics and a finite-Fourier averaging estimate; four field resonances have separate force-canceling constructions.</p><p>Six positive values of t=aκ/r remain open: 2/3, 8/3, 16/(6±√2), and 16/(4±√2). Zero stiffness instead has an exact holonomy drift counterexample. The positive-stiffness empty vacuum remains stable. 42 exact constructor checks and three numerical campaigns passed; no experimental data or physical promotion.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#nonlinear-orbital-v01">Read the theorem and its parameter boundary →</a></article><article class="claim" id="gauge-reduced-modes-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Imaginary frequencies can retain secular growth</h2><p>The exact Gauss reduction leaves 14 link oscillator pairs, 28 algebraic matter pairs and one neutral zero pair. Five positive link stiffnesses are 6, 6±√2 and 4±√2. The nonzero neutral flat